<Base
  title="Andorra family reunification (B.1) | Official Govern.ad note"
  description="Who the Government’s B.1 page says may be reunited, how long the authorisation lasts, and the means test as a multiple of the minimum wage."
  path="/family-reunification"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Family reunification (B.1)</h1>
    <p>
      The Government publishes an initial B.1 page and a B.2 renewal page.
      Who may be reunited is on those pages — not on a blog list of
      “dependants”.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Sources:
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-per-raons-de-reagrupament-autoritzacio-inicial"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          >B.1. Residència per raons de reagrupament: autorització inicial</a
        >
        and
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-per-raons-de-reagrupament-autoritzacio-de-renovacio"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">B.2 renewal</a
        >.
        A separate B.1.1 page covers reunification with heads of diplomatic
        missions. We do not invent that file. If the official text changes,
        it wins.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What B.1 authorises</h2>
    <p>
      The official page authorises the reunited person to reside in Andorra
      with an Andorran national, or with the holder of a residence-and-work
      authorisation, provided those persons reside in the country.
    </p>

    <h2>Who the official page says may apply</h2>
    <p>
      A newcomer who, under current law, is:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the spouse, or the member of a stable partnership (unió estable de parella);</li>
      <li>
        the applicant’s minor children, and the spouse’s minor children
        over whom the applicant has legal custody;
      </li>
      <li>
        the applicant’s adult children, and the spouse’s adult children,
        who are dependants and over whom the applicant exercises legal
        guardianship or a similar protective institution;
      </li>
      <li>
        dependant ascendants who are at least 65 years old or who are
        retired;
      </li>
      <li>
        other persons under the applicant’s legal guardianship or a
        similar institution recognised by Andorran law and not contrary
        to public order, where both parents have lost or had parental
        authority extinguished.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We stop there. We do not add siblings, “unmarried partners” who are
      not a registered unió estable, or any other category the page does
      not name.
    </p>

    <h2>How long the official page says it lasts</h2>
    <p>
      The maximum duration — ten years — is obtained when reunification is
      with an Andorran national.
    </p>
    <p>
      When reunification is with the holder of a residence-and-work
      authorisation, the grant is for an initial year, renewable three
      times for periods of two years. After seven years, later renewals
      are for ten years — except for nationals of states that have signed
      and ratified a convention with Andorra.
    </p>

    <h2>Means as a formula</h2>
    <p>
      The B.1 page requires evidence of the applicant’s economic means.
      Those means must represent 100% of Andorra’s minimum wage, shown
      with the last three payslips and a CASS contribution extract if the
      person is an employee, or with a bank solvency certificate, the
      CASS extract and a sworn income declaration in other cases.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same page requires the household’s combined income to be at
      least 100% of the Andorran minimum wage for each adult living in
      the home, and at least 70% of that wage for each minor. If both
      the applicant and the spouse are legal residents, Immigration
      takes both incomes into account only if the spouse expressly
      agrees. We do not convert those percentages into euros.
    </p>

    <h2>Documents listed (non-exhaustive)</h2>
    <p>
      The official list is expressly “enunciative and not limiting”. It
      includes, among other items:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the completed immigration form for non-active residents;</li>
      <li>
        passport, or — for EU/EEA nationals — a national identity card;
      </li>
      <li>
        legalised criminal-record certificates from the country of origin,
        of nationality and of residence;
      </li>
      <li>a recent colour photograph to the stated format;</li>
      <li>
        proof of accommodation (lease, title, or a public-establishment
        certificate; a domicile certificate if the dwelling is not in
        the applicant’s name);
      </li>
      <li>
        civil-status papers issued less than three months before the
        application — including, for a stable partnership, a certificate
        from the Andorran civil register;
      </li>
      <li>
        for an ascendant, health insurance covering 100% of medical
        expenses, with a sworn commitment to renew it;
      </li>
      <li>
        if the sponsor is Andorran: passport copy and parish-census
        certificate; if foreign: identity document and a copy of the
        immigration authorisation;
      </li>
      <li>
        for minor children: a birth certificate (less than three months
        old) showing filiation, or an adoption decision; custody papers
        if applicable.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Foreign official documents must carry a Hague apostille or be
      otherwise legalised. That sentence is on the same page.
    </p>

    <h2>Renewal (B.2)</h2>
    <p>
      The renewal page says the holder must have resided in Andorra
      permanently and effectively from the start of the initial
      authorisation, or from the last renewal. The application must
      reach the Servei d’Immigració before the authorisation expires.
      The listed papers include identity documents, a photograph, the
      immigration card (or a police certificate if it was lost or
      stolen), health insurance covering 100% of medical expenses for
      the life of the authorisation with a sworn commitment, and a CASS
      contribution extract for the person with whom reunification was
      made. Unnotified changes during the authorisation must be
      documented at renewal.
    </p>
    <p>
      Related:
      <a href="/become-a-resident">permit types</a>,
      <a href="/moving-to-andorra">moving to Andorra</a>,
      <a href="/tax-residence">tax residence</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

